    "case_id": "40",
    "case_summary": "A man in his mid-80s presented with a 4-year history of a left lower eyelid mass, noted by his wife to have recently grown and acquired a crust.\n\nOn examination, a yellowish-brown, well-circumscribed papule was discovered on the central lower eyelid without margin involvement. At one lateral end, the tumor had acquired a flakier surface.\n\n{'Histopathological Examination': {'Gross Examination': 'The specimen measured 0.8 × 0.5 × 0.3 cm.', 'Microscopic Findings': '- Classic-appearing hyperkeratotic lesion with many pseudohorn cysts\\n- Proliferation of neoplastic squamous cells originated from one lateral end, pushing down into the dermis and involving many hairs\\n- Malignant squamous cells contained eosinophilic cytoplasm and prominent nucleoli; mitotic figures were present\\n- Central areas of clear cell change were periodic acid–Schiff positive and diastase sensitive\\n- Negative results with Alcian blue and mucicarmine stains\\n- Negative immunohistochemical stains for adipophilin and androgen receptor\\n- Ki67 highlighted scattered basal cells and stained about 20% to 40% of tumor cell nuclei', 'Images': {'Title': 'Clinicopathologic Features', 'Description': 'Multiple microscopic images showing ulcerated lesion, tumor transition, hair involvement, Ki-67 staining patterns, and periodic acid-Schiff staining results'}}}",
